  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nRainfall Potential Overnight Into Thursday Morning:\n\nPassing low level anticyclonic flow evident on area VWPs has ushered \nin a cooler, drier airmass over the forecast area compared to \nprevious few days with the recent frontal boundary shunted just \nsouth into central Iowa. The anticyclonic flow center traverses east \nof the forecast area through today, ushering in return flow and the \naforementioned frontal boundary with accompanying increased moisture \ninto tonight. Associated precipitation chances increase locally \ntonight as mid level shear vorticity lobe and transient ageostrophic \nascent within the equatorward entrance region of a passing upper \nlevel jet streak causing meager cyclogenesis. Overall impacts \nappear minimal given diurnal timing abstaining more unstable airmass \nupstream of the forecast area into central Minnesota.\n\nHeavy Rain Friday Into Saturday:\n\nSubsequent cyclogenesis of a Rocky Mountain Low through the Northern \nPlains through Thursday returns heavy rain potential across the \nUpper Mississippi River Valley initially Friday morning. Band of \nstrong low level frontogenesis collocated with 1.5\"-2\"+ PWATs results \nin heavy rain probabilities quickly increasing through Friday \nshifting east into Friday night. Strengthening of the low through \nFriday night persists heavier rain potential into Saturday morning \nacross the forecast area. LREF (15.12Z) probabilities quickly \nincrease through Friday into Friday night with 40%/70% for 2\" of \nrainfall in 24 hrs in the GEFS/EPS. While overall placement may vary \nsome, the area of higher rainfall (2\"+) values is quite widespread. \nResultant increased concerns for river flood primarily from west-\ncentral (Black River) into central (Yellow River) and southwest \nWisconsin (Kickapoo River). As the flashiest of these rivers, \nhighest impacts would be along the Kickapoo River should this \nsolution come to fruition. While the combination of lagged \ninitialization (GEFS) with 72 hour forecast window limits \naccompanying river flooding probabilities (HEFS), GEFS rainfall \nprobabilities remain lower than EPS/GEPS, increasing flooding impact \nconcerns and probabilities above current values.\n\nEarly Next Week: Staying Cooler Than Normal, Additional Rain Chances\n\nAs the rainfall from Friday and Saturday departs the local area, \nnortheasterly flow around the surface low will likely keep \ntemperatures slightly below normal into Sunday with median high \ntemperatures in the NBM hovering in the middle 60s to near 70. The \nmain point of forecast uncertainty as we head into next week will be \nrevolving around a potential cut-off upper-level low which guidance \nhas substantial disagreements on how to handle at this point in the \nforecast cycle. Consequently, the NBM maintains some low-end \nprecipitation probabilities (15-40%) at this point from Sunday \nthrough Monday which seems reasonable given the wide range of \nsolutions with this system. However, given the presence of this low \nin the region into early next week, thinking that below normal \ntemperatures will maintain with median high temperatures in the NBM \nhovering in the upper 60s to around 70 degrees.",
